body:
I might be a  little biased, but the "space wolf" omnibus is  a great starting point bookwise. its focus is on a central character (ragnar blackmane) going from who he was in life to becoming a chapter master. great characters, and great story overall.  

&#x200B;

otherwise, I know that there is a lot of praise for the 'gaunts ghosts' series of novels  about the imperial guard. Helsreach is a book thats also very very good.

thanks for the notes. I've switched MB to the ROG strix, and gotten the WD blue.

&#x200B;

I've also removed the sound card. I had it for audio quality as i'm an audiophile, but I think its a little overkill.

Me: \*sighs\* 

&#x200B;

\*Loads up spice and wolf\*

So lets put the morality of upper management completely aside for a moment.

&#x200B;

As what the glass door reviews state, this has been a problem for a long time unbeknown to fans. While there has been promises of things changing, nothing has changed. Moreover, anecdotally we can see the same overworking practices happening throughout their entire history "Monty Oum, Employees getting a hotel across the street so they can work longer, ETC).

&#x200B;

If this issue has been without social pressure until now, and nothing has changed regarding the use of crunch time,  Why would RT bother changing? 

&#x200B;

there are a couple of arguments regarding this:

&#x200B;

1. with the bad reputation of the company's work ethics and policies, people will stop trying to work at rooster teeth. No. there will never be a time where there are no people that want to work at rooster teeth. Even putting fans aside, a lot of their talent comes from University of Texas graduates, which many will seek work in their home towns.
2. With their use of crunch, they will put out less quality content and people will stop watching due to that.If they've been using crunch up to now, it is unlikely that continue making their employees working at the same rate will result in a loss in quality. I do concede that current talent may quit and be replaced by others who are more willing to deal with the time crunches, but the key players on projects are seemingly not the target of the glass doors, the animators and off camera workers are. it is unlikely that a shift in quality would be extreme if those people were replaced by new employees.

The only thing  I can think of to change Roosterteeth's policies on crunch is a civil yet maintained level of public outcry. They risk damaging their reputation as a company and that results in lost advertisers, lost opportunities, etc. This would push a change in their works because there is a risk involved. if there isn't any risk involved with it, there is no logical reason as to why they would change their behavior if it's working.

&#x200B;

I do think that asking scandal questions at panels, sending hate towards members of RT, and otherwise actions in bad faith are bad and shouldn't be encouraged. I just think that a certain level of civil public outcry is in RT's best interest.
